T0102: FFmpeg residuals. (a) fixed, (b) refuted as a stale premise.

(a) REAL BUG, not just a leak. VideoRecorderFFmpeg is a reusable object: the
destructor and VideoRecorderThread::stop() both reach cleanup(), and a new
init() may follow on the same instance. cleanup() reset every other
per-recording resource but left hwFrame_, hwFramesCtx_, hwDeviceCtx_ alive.
A later SOFTWARE take skips initHWDevice()/initHWFrames() (gated on
settings.video.isHardwareAccelerated()), so:
  - initVideoStream() line ~1006 `if (hwFramesCtx_)` still allocates hwFrame_
    and calls av_hwframe_get_buffer() on the STALE frames context;
  - encodeVideo() line ~501 `if (hwFramesCtx_ && hwFrame_)` still calls
    av_hwframe_transfer_data() with a device/pool/dimensions belonging to the
    previous recording.
Fix (src/recorder/VideoRecorderFFmpeg.cpp, cleanup(), after the codec resets):
  hwFrame_.reset(); hwFramesCtx_.reset(); hwDeviceCtx_.reset();
  swPixelFormat_ = AV_PIX_FMT_YUV420P;
Dependency order: the frame references the frames pool, the frames context
references the device. videoCodecCtx_.reset() immediately above already drops
the codec's own hw_frames_ctx ref (codecRef from initHWFrames), so releasing
our refs afterwards is correct.

Headless verification without a GPU: two test seams in
VideoRecorderFFmpeg.hpp use av_buffer_alloc(1)/av_frame_alloc() (no device
needed, same deleters free them):
  installHardwareStateForTesting() / hardwareStateHeldForTesting()
New test hardwareStateIsNotStickyAcrossRecordings in
tests/unit/recorder/test_RecordingPipeline.cpp installs the stand-ins, calls
the REAL cleanup(), and asserts the state is gone; also asserts cleanup() is
idempotent (it is the guaranteed-terminal path on a failed init and at
destruction). Confirmed present in the generated
test_RecordingPipeline.moc and selected cleanly within TestRecorder.

(b) STALE PREMISE. `grep -rn "read_packet|AVERROR_EXIT|avio_alloc_context"
src/ tests/ cmake/` returns only the explanatory comment at
VideoRecorderFFmpeg.cpp:142, which states no AVIO context is used: the muxer
writes discrete packets and the demuxer read-callback idiom does not apply.
There is no custom AVIO and no worker join to signal, so there is nothing to
implement. Consistent with the T0104/T0090 stale-premise closures.

